<h1>Vertical Lift &amp; Box Elevators</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A vertical lift conveyor moves cases, cartons, totes, and other discrete products straight up or down between floor levels or equipment elevations, using far less floor space than an incline conveyor. Continuous vertical conveyors (CVCs) circulate multiple carriers in a loop for steady case flow. Vertical reciprocating conveyors (VRCs) shuttle a single carriage between levels for heavy or low-volume loads. Spirals and steep Z-frame belt conveyors cover the same elevation job with different trade-offs.</p>
<h2 id="why-heading">Why Vertical Lift</h2>
<p>Packaging lines often need to move product between mezzanine levels, over obstructions, or from equipment at one height to equipment at another. <a href="/conveyor-types/incline-conveyors/">Incline conveyors</a> do this but consume floor space. The horizontal run equals the rise divided by the tangent of the incline angle: a 10-foot rise at 20 degrees needs about 27 feet of run, and at 30 degrees about 17 feet. <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/solutions/cleated-belt-conveyor-solutions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dorner</a> notes that flat belts are often limited to less than 20 degrees, which makes the run even longer.</p>
<p>A true vertical lift removes most of that horizontal run. Its footprint is set by product size and carrier design, and it grows little as the rise increases. That floor space saving often justifies the higher equipment cost in plants where production floor is scarce.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/vertical-lift-roach-cvc.webp" width="1000" height="750" alt="A tall green vertical conveyor tower with orange mesh guarding and a short belt infeed conveyor entering at floor level." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A Roach continuous vertical conveyor (CVC): product enters on the belt infeed at floor level and rides carriers up inside the guarded tower, so the footprint stays small as the rise increases. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://roachconveyors.com/conveyor_solutions/specialty_conveyors/vertical_lift_conveyors/cvc/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Roach Conveyors</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<p>Vertical lifts with carriers or platforms also keep product level and oriented, which matters for downstream <a href="/conveyor-functions/tray-loading/">tray loading</a>, <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">collating</a>, and case packing.</p>
<h2 id="types-heading">Vertical Lift Types</h2>
<h3>Continuous Vertical Conveyor (CVC or CVL)</h3>
<p>A continuous vertical conveyor circulates multiple carriers or platforms on chains in a loop. Carriers rise on one side and return on the other, so a new carrier reaches the load point without waiting for a return trip. Throughput depends on the number of carriers and chain speed. <a href="https://www.qimarox.com/product/prorunner-mk5/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Qimarox</a> rates its Prorunner mk5 at up to 2,000 products per hour, with a maximum of 110 lbs per carrier and a highest infeed or outfeed position of 757 inches. <a href="https://roachconveyors.com/conveyor_solutions/specialty_conveyors/vertical_lift_conveyors/cvc/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Roach</a> rates its CVC at up to 25 loads per minute and up to 3,000 lbs at a standard 60 FPM.</p>
<p>CVCs need infeed control that matches the carrier arrival rate. Timing or <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing conveyors</a> at the infeed meter product so each item meets an empty carrier. Poor timing causes jams or missed carriers.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/vertical-lift-qimarox-prorunner-mk5.webp" width="1000" height="563" alt="Rendering of a slim red and silver vertical conveyor column with fork-style carriers holding boxes at several heights and roller conveyors at the bottom and top." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Rendering of the Qimarox Prorunner mk5 continuous vertical conveyor: carriers circulate on the column and pick up boxes from the lower roller conveyor and deliver them to the upper one. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.qimarox.com/product/prorunner-mk5/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Qimarox</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>C-Flow and Z-Flow Configurations</h3>
<p>CVCs are built in two basic flow patterns. Roach defines them this way:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>C-flow:</strong> Product enters and exits on the same side of the unit, with consistent entry and exit orientation. Use it when infeed and outfeed conveyors are on the same side.</li>
<li><strong>Z-flow:</strong> Product enters at one level and exits on the opposite side at the other level. Use it when infeed and outfeed approach from opposite directions.</li>
</ul>
<p>Qimarox offers its mk5 in multiple C and Z combinations, including front-in and side-out layouts. Choose the flow pattern from your conveyor layout before you compare models.</p>
<h3>Z-Frame Cleated Belt Conveyor</h3>
<p>A Z-frame belt conveyor has a horizontal infeed, a steep cleated incline, and a horizontal outfeed at the upper level. It is a steep incline, not a true vertical lift. Dorner states that a cleated belt can incline at angles up to 60 degrees, and its LPZ Z-frame models are offered in fixed angles within that limit. Cleats or sidewalls hold product during the climb.</p>
<p>Z-frames handle small parts, cases, bags, and pouches. Because they use a single belt drive instead of a chain-and-carrier system, they are usually simpler and lower cost than a CVC. Product transfers onto the flat infeed at normal belt speed, so no carrier timing is needed. The trade-off is a longer footprint than a vertical lift and less control of product orientation.</p>
<h3>Vertical Reciprocating Conveyor (VRC)</h3>
<p>A VRC uses a single carriage that shuttles between two or more levels. It loads, travels, stops, unloads, and returns, so throughput is set by full cycle time. PFlow, for example, rates its <a href="https://www.pflow.com/vertical-conveyors/hydraulic/21-series/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">21 Series</a> hydraulic VRC at 10 cycles per hour standard, while mechanical VRCs are offered for higher-cycle automated duty. VRCs carry the heaviest loads of any type on this page, including full pallets and carts.</p>
<p>VRCs are classified as conveyors under <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a>, not elevators, as long as they carry only materials. See the <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">VRC page</a> for capacities, mast configurations, and code requirements.</p>
<h3>Spiral Conveyors</h3>
<p>A <a href="/conveyor-types/spiral-conveyors/">spiral conveyor</a> carries product on a continuous slat or belt surface in a helix around a central column. Product rides on a flat surface with no carrier transfers. <a href="https://ryson.com/spiral-conveyors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ryson</a> lists speeds up to 200 FPM with a variable frequency drive, and outside diameters from about 4 feet (narrow 6-inch slat) to 13 feet (36-inch slat), depending on model.</p>
<p>A spiral takes more floor space than a CVC because of its helix diameter, but far less than an incline. Spirals can also hold product during an elevation change and act as a buffer when the downstream line stops. See <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation conveyors</a>.</p>
