<p>Rates and capacities vary widely by model. Use the manufacturer figures cited above as examples, and get ratings for your product. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>.</p>
<h2 id="integration-heading">Packaging Line Integration</h2>
<h3>Infeed Conveyor Requirements</h3>
<p>CVCs need product timing at the infeed. Product must reach the load point when an empty carrier arrives. A timing conveyor or servo-driven <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing conveyor</a> upstream creates the spacing. Sensors detect carrier position and signal the infeed to release the next product.</p>
<p>VRCs have simpler infeed requirements because the carriage stops at the load level. A transport conveyor pushes product onto the stopped carriage, a sensor confirms presence, and the lift begins its travel.</p>
<h3>Outfeed and Downstream Handling</h3>
<p>CVCs and spirals deliver product continuously, so the outfeed conveyor must clear product at least as fast as it arrives. VRCs deliver in batches, and the outfeed must clear the carriage before it can return. An <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation conveyor</a> downstream of a VRC absorbs the intermittent delivery.</p>
<h3>Controls Integration</h3>
<p>Vertical lift controls must communicate with the line PLC to coordinate start, stop, speed changes, and faults. Most lift manufacturers supply a lift control panel that interfaces to the line PLC by discrete I/O (start, stop, fault, carrier-at-level) or an industrial Ethernet protocol such as EtherNet/IP. The lift controller handles positioning, drive speed, and safety interlocks. The line PLC handles product flow, infeed timing, and outfeed coordination.</p>
<p>Safety devices include position sensors, overtravel limits, chain break or slack chain detection, and access door or gate switches. Opening an access door stops the lift. ASME B20.1 and local codes set the required safety devices. VRCs have specific gate and enclosure requirements, covered on the <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">VRC page</a>.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Vertical Lift Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Mezzanine level changes:</strong> Moving cases from ground-floor packaging to mezzanine palletizing, or from mezzanine production to ground-floor shipping. CVCs and spirals handle continuous case flow. VRCs handle pallets and lower-volume transfers.</p>
<p><strong>Over-conveyor bridges:</strong> Lifting product over a perpendicular conveyor line, walkway, or forklift aisle, then lowering it on the other side. A pair of vertical lifts with a horizontal bridge conveyor keeps floor-level traffic moving.</p>
<p><strong>End-of-line case elevation:</strong> Sealed cases from a <a href="/conveyor-functions/slug-loading/">case packer</a> elevate to palletizer infeed height, or to an overhead conveyor that carries them to a central palletizing area. A CVC or spiral provides continuous flow without a long incline run.</p>
<p><strong>Multi-level distribution:</strong> Distribution centers with pick modules on multiple levels use vertical lifts to move totes and cases between levels. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> on each level feed product to the lift, which delivers it to the shipping sortation level.</p>
<p><strong>Bulk product elevation:</strong> Granular, loose, and bulk products cannot ride a carrier. <a href="/conveyor-types/flighted-scoop-conveyors/">Flighted and scoop conveyors</a> or <a href="/conveyor-types/bucket-elevators/">bucket elevators</a> handle that job.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">Selecting a Vertical Lift</h2>
<p>Match the lift type to these parameters:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Throughput.</strong> Continuous flow points to a CVC or spiral. Moderate flow of small items can fit a Z-frame. Batch loads of a few cycles per hour fit a VRC. Calculate your peak rate and confirm it against the manufacturer's rating.</li>
<li><strong>Elevation change.</strong> Check the maximum rise for each model. CVCs and mechanical VRCs are offered for multi-floor rises. Hydraulic VRCs and Z-frames suit shorter rises. A spiral adds a turn for each increment of rise, so footprint stays the same but height and cost grow.</li>
<li><strong>Product weight and size.</strong> CVCs have a per-carrier weight limit and a product size window (for example, Qimarox lists 110 lbs per carrier). VRCs handle pallets and the heaviest loads. Z-frames suit light items held by cleats.</li>
<li><strong>Layout.</strong> Infeed and outfeed on the same side points to C-flow. Opposite sides points to Z-flow. Confirm overhead clearance and floor openings.</li>
<li><strong>Floor space.</strong> CVCs use the least floor space for vertical-only movement. Z-frames use more because of the incline run and flat sections. Spirals need room for the helix diameter.</li>
<li><strong>Cost drivers.</strong> Rise, throughput (carrier count or turns), product size and weight, sanitary or washdown construction, and controls integration. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>.</li>
</ol>
