<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a vertical lift conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A vertical lift conveyor moves products up or down between elevations on carriers, platforms, or carriages in a vertical path. Types include continuous vertical conveyors (CVCs) in C-flow or Z-flow configurations, VRCs, and spirals. Steep Z-frame cleated belt conveyors are a related option. All use much less floor space than an incline conveyor.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">CVL vs. VRC, what is the difference?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>CVCs circulate multiple carriers continuously. VRCs shuttle a single carriage with stops at each level, so throughput is set by cycle time. Qimarox rates its Prorunner mk5 CVC at up to 2,000 products per hour, while PFlow rates its 21 Series hydraulic VRC at 10 cycles per hour standard. VRCs carry much heavier loads, including full pallets.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How much floor space does a vertical lift require?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A vertical lift footprint is set by product size and carrier design and grows little with rise. An incline needs a run equal to rise divided by the tangent of the angle: about 27 feet for a 10-foot rise at 20 degrees. Spirals need room for the helix, which Ryson lists from about 4 to 13 feet in diameter by model.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What products can vertical lifts handle?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Cases, cartons, totes, trays, bundles, and pallets. Products should have a stable flat bottom. CVCs have a per-carrier limit (110 lbs on the Qimarox Prorunner mk5). VRCs handle pallets, with Autoquip rating hydraulic units up to 6,000 lbs and mechanical units up to 20,000 lbs. Loose, granular, or bulk products need <a href="/conveyor-types/flighted-scoop-conveyors/">flighted or scoop conveyors</a> instead.</p>

</div>
</div>
